---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
import Main from "@/components/Main.astro";
import config from "@/config";
---

<Layout
  title={`Scroll Budget Privacy Policy | ${config.site.title}`}
  description="How Scroll Budget processes website budgets and browsing activity locally on your device."
>
  <Header />
  <Main pageTitle="Scroll Budget Privacy Policy" class="app-prose">
    <p>
      Effective date: October 7, 2026<br />
      Applies to: Scroll Budget, version 1.0.0<br />
      Developer: erdemtuna
    </p>
    <p>
      Scroll Budget helps you set daily time and hourly or daily visit budgets
      for websites and website sections. It processes browser activity locally
      to provide that feature. The extension does not send your rules, browsing
      activity, or usage to the developer or to a server.
    </p>
    <h2>Information processed on your device</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your settings:</strong> website domains and optional path prefixes,
        enabled/paused rules, time and visit budgets, frequency units, and enforcement
        modes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Usage counters:</strong> foreground time and visit counts for each
        rule, together with the current local day and hour used to reset those counters.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Current browser activity:</strong> top-level navigation URLs, tab
        identifiers, the current active tab, page visibility, and whether Chrome's
        window is focused or minimized. Chrome navigation events can reveal URLs for
        tabs outside your selected rules as well. The extension uses those events
        to distinguish entering and leaving rules, but does not retain unrelated URLs.
        Leaving every active permitted rule clears that tab's tracked state.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Temporary tracked-tab state:</strong> rule membership, whether a visit
        was admitted, temporary override flags, and locally generated visit/gate tokens.
        Current matching URLs are held in the live background worker's memory, not
        persisted for admitted visits. A full original URL is kept in session storage
        only while a matching tracked visit has a pending limit page, so a permitted
        retry or override can return to the right page. Full return URLs can include
        query strings and fragments; a website may put personal or sensitive information
        in those parts of its URL.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The extension does not read page text, form values, messages, account
      credentials, cookies, payment information, or downloaded files. Its
      content script reports visibility and displays its own warning notice; it
      does not scrape the site's content. The word <code>continue</code> is checked
      to authorize an override and is not a password or an account credential.
    </p>
    <h2>Why this information is used</h2>
    <p>
      The information is used only to match your website rules, count visits and
      focused foreground time, apply your chosen warning/override/blocking mode,
      display your budgets, reset counters, and return the same tab to its
      original page after an eligible retry or deliberate override. Temporary
      state lets an admitted visit survive a page reload or extension-worker
      suspension without becoming another visit.
    </p>
    <h2>Storage and retention</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Rules and current per-rule counters are stored in Chrome's <code
          >storage.local</code
        > on your device. They are not stored in <code>storage.sync</code>.
        Usage buckets reset when the extension reconciles the new local day or
        hour; no long-term usage timeline is kept. Settings remain until you
        edit/delete them or uninstall the extension.
      </li>
      <li>
        Minimal tracked-tab state and pending-gate return URLs are stored in
        Chrome's <code>storage.session</code>. Untracked URLs are never retained
        there. Leaving all active permitted rules, closing the tab, or Chrome
        clearing extension session storage removes the tab state; Chrome clears
        session storage on browser restart, extension reload/update, or
        disabling the extension. Returning from a gate removes its stored full
        URL. Background-worker suspension alone does not clear a pending gate or
        admitted visit; the worker recovers current matching URLs from Chrome
        when it restarts.
      </li>
      <li>
        Chrome manages extension storage and site-access grants. Scroll Budget
        does not add application-level encryption to local storage. Anyone with
        sufficient access to your browser profile or computer may be able to
        inspect local extension data.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Sharing, transmission, and third parties</h2>
    <p>
      Scroll Budget has no account, backend, analytics, advertising, telemetry,
      remote scripts, or data-sale service. Its runtime messages pass between
      extension components within your browser, not to an external service. The
      developer does not receive or inspect your rules or browsing usage through
      the extension.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you choose to visit or return to a website, Chrome makes the ordinary
      website request, which is governed by that website's practices. Chrome and
      the Chrome Web Store may process installation, update, or store-support
      information under Google's policies independently of Scroll Budget. If you
      voluntarily contact the developer through store support, the information
      you choose to send is handled by that support service and used to respond;
      avoid including sensitive URLs or personal information.
    </p>
    <h2>Your controls</h2>
    <p>
      You can view, edit, pause, or delete rules in <strong>Manage rules</strong
      >. Deleting a rule deletes its stored per-rule usage. Pausing a rule stops
      its budget tracking but keeps the rule and counters. Chrome's extension
      site-access settings let you revoke a site's permission; a rule without
      permission is labeled <strong>Needs site permission</strong> and does not enforce
      tracking. Existing Chrome grants are not automatically removed when you pause
      or delete a rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      Leaving a rule or closing its tab ends the current override. Restarting
      Chrome clears temporary session state. Uninstalling Scroll Budget removes
      its extension storage under Chrome's normal behavior. Incognito is not
      supported.
    </p>
    <h2>Limited Use</h2>
    <p>
      Scroll Budget's use of information received from Chrome APIs adheres to
      the Chrome Web Store User Data Policy, including the Limited Use
      requirements. Browser activity is used only for the prominently described
      budgeting feature. It is not used or transferred for advertising, sold to
      third parties, or used to determine creditworthiness or lending
      eligibility. The extension does not make your browser activity available
      for human review by the developer.
    </p>
    <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
    <p>
      This policy will be updated if the extension's data practices change. The
      effective date and applicable version identify this policy. The canonical
      policy location is <a href="https://erdemtuna.dev/scroll-budget/privacy/"
        >erdemtuna.dev/scroll-budget/privacy/</a
      >. For questions, use the contact/support channel on Scroll Budget's
      Chrome Web Store listing once published. The developer's identity is <a
        href="https://github.com/erdemtuna">erdemtuna on GitHub</a
      >; the source repository is private and is not a public support channel.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also contact the developer through <a
        href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/erdem-tuna/">LinkedIn</a
      >.
    </p>
  </Main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
